Input name collisions don't directly impact Angular bundle size. The naming convention has no measurable effect on:

1. Bundle size - identical regardless of naming
2. Tree-shaking - unaffected by input names
3. Change detection performance - same efficiency
4. Runtime overhead - negligible difference

The main impact is on code maintainability and debugging time, not bundle metrics.

```typescript
// Both have identical bundle size
declare class ComponentA {
  title: string; // Clear naming
}

declare class ComponentB {
  myTitle: string; // Also works
}
```

Recommendation: Focus on naming conventions for clarity rather than performance metrics. Bundle optimization should target other areas like lazy loading and code splitting.

