The `<ng-content>` element is a special placeholder, not a DOM element or component. Angular's compiler processes all instances at build-time to determine where content should be projected. This means you cannot conditionally include `<ng-content>` with `@if`, `@for`, or `@switch` because Angular always instantiates and creates DOM nodes for content rendered to a placeholder, even if that placeholder is hidden.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-container',
  template: `
    <div class="wrapper">
      <!-- Correct: Always include ng-content -->
      <ng-content></ng-content>
    </div>
  `,
})
export class ContainerComponent {}
```

```typescript
// Incorrect: Do not use @if with ng-content
@Component({
  selector: 'app-bad-container',
  template: `
    <div class="wrapper">
      @if (showContent) {
        <ng-content></ng-content>
      }
    </div>
  `,
})
export class BadContainerComponent {}
```

For conditional rendering of component content, use Template fragments instead:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-dynamic',
  template: `
    <div class="wrapper">
      @if (showContent) {
        <ng-template #contentTemplate>
          <p>Dynamic content</p>
        </ng-template>
        <ng-container [ngTemplateOutlet]="contentTemplate"></ng-container>
      }
    </div>
  `,
})
export class DynamicComponent {}
```

Key constraints:
- Cannot insert, remove, or modify `<ng-content>` at run time
- Cannot add directives, styles, or arbitrary attributes to `<ng-content>`
- Always creates DOM nodes for projected content regardless of visibility state

Implement the ngOnInit() and ngOnDestroy() lifecycle hooks as methods on your component class:

ngOnInit(): void {
  // Run initialization code here
  console.log('Component initialized');
}

ngOnDestroy(): void {
  // Cleanup logic before destruction
  console.log('Component destroyed');
  // Clear subscriptions, timers, etc.
}

Angular traverses your application tree from top to bottom, checking template bindings for changes. This traversal visits each component exactly once. During this process:

1. Avoid making further state changes in the middle of the process
2. Lifecycle hooks run while Angular is doing this traversal
3. Each step represents a different part of Angular's rendering and update-checking process

Example of what to avoid:

ngOnInit(): void {
  // DON'T modify component state here during traversal
  this.data = fetchData(); // May cause issues if called mid-traversal
}

Lifecycle hooks relate to specific component instances are implemented as methods on your component class. Lifecycle hooks that relate to the Angular application as a whole are implemented as functions that accept a callback.

Component instance hooks (methods):
ngOnInit(): void {
  // Runs for this specific component instance
}

Application-wide hooks (functions with callbacks):
function appInit(callback: () => void) {
  // Runs once for the entire application
  callback();
}

Use methods when you need per-component logic, use functions with callbacks for global application lifecycle events.

`ngOnInit` runs once after Angular has initialized all the component's inputs. Use it for initialization logic that depends on input values. `ngAfterViewInit` runs once after the component's view has been initialized. Use it when you need to access DOM elements or query selectors within the template.

```typescript
export class MyComponent implements OnInit, AfterViewInit {
  // Access input data here
  ngOnInit() {
    console.log('Inputs initialized');
  }

  // Access DOM elements here
  ngAfterViewInit() {
    const el = this.elementRef.nativeElement.querySelector('.my-element');
    console.log('View rendered');
  }
}
```

You must unsubscribe from observables in `ngOnDestroy`, which runs once before the component is destroyed. Failure to do so causes memory leaks.

```typescript
import { Component, OnDestroy } from '@angular/core';
import { Subscription } from 'rxjs';

export class MyComponent implements OnDestroy {
  private subscription: Subscription;

  ngOnInit() {
    this.subscription = someService.getData().subscribe(data => {
      console.log(data);
    });
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    if (this.subscription) {
      this.subscription.unsubscribe();
    }
  }
}
```

`ngOnChanges` runs every time the component's inputs have changed, specifically before change detection. Use it to react to bound input properties. `ngDoCheck` runs every time this component is checked for changes, regardless of inputs. Use it for custom change detection logic like monitoring Signals or third-party libraries.

```typescript
import { SimpleChanges } from '@angular/core';

export class MyComponent implements OnChanges, DoCheck {
  ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges) {
    if (changes.myInput) {
      console.log('Input changed');
    }
  }

  ngDoCheck() {
    // Custom logic every cycle
    this.checkCustomState();
  }
}
```

Yes, ngOnInit is the standard hook for initialization logic after Angular has set initial input values. It runs before the template is initialized, allowing you to prepare state based on those inputs.

Here is the implementation:
```typescript
export class UserComponent implements OnInit {
  @Input() userId!: string;
  private user: any;

  constructor(private userService: UserService) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    if (this.userId) {
      this.userService.getUser(this.userId).subscribe(data => {
        this.user = data;
      });
    }
  }
}
```

No, ngOnInit runs exactly once per component instance. It is not triggered by subsequent input changes. For dynamic updates based on input modifications, you should implement the OnChanges interface.

Here is the pattern:
```typescript
export class UserComponent implements OnInit, OnChanges {
  @Input() userId!: string;
  private user: any;

  constructor(private userService: UserService) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // Initial load logic
  }

  ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges): void {
    if (changes['userId']) {
      this.userService.getUser(this.userId).subscribe(data => {
        this.user = data;
      });
    }
  }
}
```

ngOnInit executes before the component's own template is initialized. This means you can update the component's state based on its initial input values prior to view rendering.

Here is the execution order context:
```typescript
export class DashboardComponent implements OnInit {
  @Input() config!: any;

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // State preparation happens here
    console.log('Template will render after this step');
  }
}
```

The ngOnChanges method runs after component inputs change but before the template is checked. During initialization, the first run occurs before ngOnInit. You can access previous values, current values, and a flag for initial changes using the SimpleChange object.

```ts
@Component({
  /* ... */
})
export class UserProfile {
  name = input('');

  ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges<UserProfile>) {
    if (changes.name) {
      console.log(`Previous: ${changes.name.previousValue}`);
      console.log(`Current: ${changes.name.currentValue}`);
      console.log(`Is first: ${changes.name.firstChange}`);
    }
  }
}
```

Input aliases do not change the keys in the SimpleChanges Record; it always uses the TypeScript property name. You can enforce stronger type checking by passing the current class as the first generic argument for SimpleChanges.

```ts
ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges<UserProfile>) {
  // Keys will be 'name' regardless of alias
}
```

Use ngAfterContentInit to read content query results without modifying state:

```typescript
import { Component, ContentChildren, QueryList } from '@angular/core';
import { ChildComponent } from './child.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  template: `<ng-content></ng-content>`
})
export class ParentComponent {
  @ContentChildren(ChildComponent) children!: QueryList<ChildComponent>;

  ngAfterContentInit() {
    // Read the initialized content query results
    console.log('Projected children:', this.children);

    // Do NOT modify state here - this causes ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError
    // Instead, use ngOnChanges or a separate method for modifications
  }
}
```

If you need to trigger side effects based on content, implement a separate method and call it from ngAfterContentInit:

```typescript
ngAfterContentInit() {
  this.initializeChildren();
}

private initializeChildren() {
  // Safe state modifications in a separate method
  this.children.forEach(child => child.init());
}

Use ngAfterContentInit when working with projected content (ng-content) and ContentQuery decorators. Use ngAfterViewInit for direct DOM element access via ViewChild:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterContentInit, AfterViewInit, ContentChildren, QueryList, ViewChild } from '@angular/core';
import { ChildComponent } from './child.component';
import { ElementRef } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-mixed',
  template: `
    <ng-content></ng-content>
    <div #hostElement>Host element</div>
  `,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush
})
export class MixedComponent implements AfterContentInit, AfterViewInit {
  @ContentChildren(ChildComponent) projectedChildren!: QueryList<ChildComponent>;
  @ViewChild('hostElement') hostElement!: ElementRef;

  ngAfterContentInit() {
    // Access ContentQuery results (projected content)
    console.log('Projected:', this.projectedChildren);
  }

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    // Access DOM elements directly
    console.log('Host element:', this.hostElement.nativeElement);
  }
}
```

Key distinction: ContentQueries work with projected nodes, ViewQueries work with template-defined elements.

Separate state modifications from lifecycle hook execution:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterContentInit, ContentChildren, QueryList } from '@angular/core';
import { ChildComponent } from './child.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-safe',
  template: `<ng-content></ng-content>`
})
export class SafeComponent implements AfterContentInit {
  @ContentChildren(ChildComponent) children!: QueryList<ChildComponent>;
  private initialized = false;

  ngAfterContentInit() {
    // Read content query results safely
    this.children.forEach(child => {
      if (!child.initialized) {
        child.init();
      }
    });

    // Mark as done reading - no state changes here
    this.readContent = true;
  }

  private readContent = false;

  onUserAction() {
    // Safe state modifications in regular methods
    if (this.readContent && this.children.length > 0) {
      this.children.forEach(child => child.process());
    }
  }
}
```

The pattern: Read in lifecycle hook, modify in component methods. This prevents ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError by keeping change detection and state updates separate.

Use ngAfterViewInit to read initialized view query results, but avoid modifying state in this lifecycle hook:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterViewInit, ViewChild, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `
    <div #content>Hello</div>
  `
})
export class ExampleComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChild('content') contentRef!: ElementRef;

  ngAfterViewInit(): void {
    // Safe to read the initialized element
    const element = this.contentRef.nativeElement;
    console.log(element.textContent);

    // Initialize third-party libraries here
    // Do NOT modify component state or trigger change detection
  }
}
```

The key principle is: read query results, don't write to them. Any state modifications should happen in ngOnInit or after the view cycle completes.

Use ngAfterViewInit specifically when you need to access initialized children in the template. Here's the proper structure:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterViewInit, ViewChild, QueryList, ViewChildren } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-list',
  template: `
    <div *ngFor="let item of items">
      <span #label>{{ item.name }}</span>
    </div>
  `
})
export class ListComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  @ViewChildren('label') labels!: QueryList<ElementRef>;

  ngAfterViewInit(): void {
    // Access all initialized child elements
    this.labels.forEach((ref, index) => {
      const span = ref.nativeElement;
      console.log(`Label ${index}:`, span.textContent);

      // Apply styles or initialize plugins
      span.style.color = 'blue';
    });
  }

  items = [
    { name: 'Item 1' },
    { name: 'Item 2' }
  ];
}
```

Lifecycle order for DOM access:
1. ngOnInit - Initialize data, not DOM
2. ngAfterViewInit - Read initialized view queries, safe DOM manipulation
3. ngOnDestroy - Cleanup resources

This error occurs because Angular checks change detection before running lifecycle hooks. Avoid modifying component state in ngAfterViewInit:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterViewInit, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-safe',
  template: `
    <div [ngClass]="className">Content</div>
  `
})
export class SafeComponent implements OnInit {
  className = '';
  data = {};

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // Initialize state here - safe for change detection
    this.className = 'active';
    this.data = { value: 1 };
  }

  ngAfterViewInit(): void {
    // Only read initialized view queries, don't modify state
    const element = document.querySelector('.active');
    console.log(element?.classList.contains('active')); // Safe read

    // If you need to update state after view initialization:
    // Use setTimeout or change detection triggers
    setTimeout(() => {
      this.className = 'updated'; // This will trigger another check
    }, 0);
  }
}
```

Correct pattern: Initialize data in ngOnInit, read DOM queries in ngAfterViewInit. For post-initialization state updates, use async patterns or manual change detection triggers.

This error occurs because Angular has already checked bindings before this hook runs. Mutating state here triggers the error. To fix this, move the logic to `ngDoCheck` or restructure your component. Here is a safe pattern using `ngDoCheck`:

```typescript
import { Component, DoCheck, ViewQuery } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: '<div>{{ value }}</div>'
})
export class ExampleComponent implements DoCheck {
  value: string = '';
  private query!: ViewQuery;

  ngDoCheck(): void {
    if (this.query) {
      this.value = this.query.result?.data || '';
    }
  }
}
```

Implementing these interfaces ensures your method signatures match exactly, preventing typos or misspellings. For example:

```ts
@Component({
  /* ... */
})
export class UserProfile implements OnInit {
  ngOnInit() {
    /* ... */
  }
}
```

Remove the 'ng' prefix from the lifecycle hook name. The interface is simply OnDestroy.

```ts
export class MyComponent implements OnDestroy {
  ngOnDestroy() {
    /* cleanup logic */
  }
}
```

Yes, you can list multiple interfaces separated by commas in the implements clause.

```ts
export class DashboardComponent implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  ngOnInit() {}
  ngOnDestroy() {}
}
```

No, you cannot rely on the execution order. When you put one or more directives on the same element as a component, either in a template or with the hostDirectives property, the framework does not guarantee any ordering of a given lifecycle hook between the component and the directives on a single element.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-component',
  hostDirectives: [MyDirective]
})
export class MyComponent {}
```
Never depend on an observed ordering, as this may change in later versions of Angular.

You can create a binding by using double curly-braces around the signal function call. This ensures changes to the component's data automatically update the rendered template.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'user-profile',
  template: "<h1>Profile for {{ userName() }}</h1>",
})
export class UserProfile {
  userName = signal('pro_programmer_123');
}
```

Use property binding with square brackets for DOM properties. Define the signal in your component class and bind it in the template:
```typescript
@Component({
  template: `<button [disabled]='!isValidUserId()'>Save</button>`,
})
export class UserProfile {
  isValidUserId = signal(false);
}
