Angular automatically updates the DOM property when the bound value changes.

Use the @if block with an optional @else clause to handle both states:

```html
<h1>User profile</h1>

@if (isAdmin()) {
  <h2>Admin settings</h2>
  <button class="admin-btn">Manage Users</button>
  <button class="admin-btn">View Logs</button>
} @else {
  <h2>User settings</h2>
  <button class="user-btn">Edit Profile</button>
  <button class="user-btn">Change Password</button>
}
```

The `isAdmin()` signal returns true when the user has admin privileges, rendering the admin-specific content. When false, the @else block displays regular user settings instead.

Use the @for directive to iterate over an array with proper tracking:

```html
<ul class="user-badge-list">
  @for (badge of badges(); track badge.id) {
    <li class="user-badge" title="{{ badge.name }}">{{ badge.name }}</li>
  }
</ul>
```

The `track` keyword associates data with DOM elements by providing a unique identifier (badge.id in this case). Without tracking, Angular must compare entire objects to detect changes, which is inefficient. With tracking, Angular only compares the track value, enabling faster change detection and preventing unnecessary re-rendering when badge properties change but IDs remain stable.

Combine both directives by nesting them within the same template:

```html
<h1>User profile</h1>

@if (isAdmin()) {
  <h2>Admin settings</h2>
  <button class="admin-btn">Manage Users</button>
} @else {
  <h2>User settings</h2>
  <button class="user-btn">Edit Profile</button>
}

<ul class="user-badge-list">
  @for (badge of badges(); track badge.id) {
    <li class="user-badge">{{ badge.name }}</li>
  }
</ul>
```

The @if block handles conditional admin controls at the top, while the @for directive renders the badge list below. Both directives operate independently and can be placed anywhere in the template hierarchy.

To bind a native event like `click` in Angular templates, wrap the event name in parentheses `()` and assign it to a method. This creates an event listener that triggers whenever the element emits that event.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <button (click)="handleClick()">Submit</button>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  handleClick(): void {
    console.log('Button clicked');
  }
}
```

Yes, you can add listeners for any native events by repeating the binding syntax for each event type. Angular supports standard DOM events like `keydown`, `keyup`, `mouseover`, `click`, etc.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-input',
  template: `
    <input type="text" (keydown)="onKeyDown()" (mouseover)="onMouseOver()" />
  `,
})
export class InputComponent {
  onKeyDown(): void {
    console.log('Key pressed');
  }

  onMouseOver(): void {
    console.log('Input focused');
  }
}
```

Angular automatically provides a variable named $event within every template event listener. You pass this to your method argument. Here is the implementation:

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <input type="text" (keyup)="updateField($event)" />
  `,
})
export class App {
  updateField(event: KeyboardEvent): void {
    console.log(`The user pressed: ${event.key}`);
  }
}
```

You should use KeyboardEvent to ensure type safety. The method signature must declare this type explicitly.

```typescript
updateField(event: KeyboardEvent): void {
  console.log(`The user pressed: ${event.key}`);
}
```

You can use the key modifier syntax directly in the event binding. This reduces boilerplate code by filtering events at the template level.

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <input type="text" (keyup.enter)="updateField($event)" />
  `,
  ...
})
export class App {
  updateField(event: KeyboardEvent): void {
    console.log('The user pressed enter in the text field.');
  }
}
```

To listen for global events like clicks on the entire window, use the host binding configuration within your @Component decorator. You can prefix event names with window:, document:, or body: to delegate listeners to these specific DOM targets.

```typescript
@Component({
  host: {
    'window:click': 'onWindowClick()',
    'document:keydown': 'onDocumentKeydown()'
  }
})
export class MyView {}
```

The framework supports exactly three global target names for prefixing events in host bindings. These targets allow you to intercept interactions at the DOM level rather than just within your component's view container. The supported targets are window, document, and body.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-view',
  host: {
    'window:beforeunload': 'onBeforeUnload()',
    'body:scroll': 'onBodyScroll()'
  }
})
export class MyView {}
```

To implement a custom event plugin with debouncing, extend the EventManagerPlugin class and override the required methods:

```ts
import {Injectable} from '@angular/core';
import {EventManagerPlugin} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Injectable()
export class DebounceEventPlugin extends EventManagerPlugin {
  constructor() {
    super(document);
  }

  override supports(eventName: string) {
    return /debounce/.test(eventName);
  }

  override addEventListener(element: HTMLElement, eventName: string, handler: Function) {
    const [event, method, delay = 300] = eventName.split('.');
    let timeoutId: number;

    const listener = (event: Event) => {
      clearTimeout(timeoutId);
      timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
        handler(event);
      }, delay);
    };

    element.addEventListener(event, listener);

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timeoutId);
      element.removeEventListener(event, listener);
    };
  }
}
```

The supports method identifies events matching the debounce pattern (e.g., click.debounce.500), while addEventListener parses the event name to extract the base event and delay value.

Register your custom plugin by providing it through the EVENT_MANAGER_PLUGINS token with multi: true flag:

```ts
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {EVENT_MANAGER_PLUGINS} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {App} from './app';
import {DebounceEventPlugin} from './debounce-event-plugin';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    {
      provide: EVENT_MANAGER_PLUGINS,
      useClass: DebounceEventPlugin,
      multi: true,
    },
  ],
});
```

The multi: true flag is essential because EVENT_MANAGER_PLUGINS expects multiple values. The useClass option creates a new instance of your plugin, and Angular will merge it with existing event manager plugins.

Once registered, you can use custom event syntax in templates with parentheses for output bindings and in host properties:

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <input
      type="text"
      (input.debounce.500)="onSearch($event.target.value)"
      placeholder="Search..."
    />
  `,
})
export class Search {
  onSearch(query: string): void {
    console.log('Searching for:', query);
  }
}
```

For host properties, use the host configuration:

```ts
@Component({
  host: {
    '(click.debounce.500)': 'handleDebouncedClick()',
  },
})
export class AwesomeCard {
  handleDebouncedClick(): void {
    console.log('Debounced click!');
  }
}
```

The syntax follows the pattern: baseEvent.method.delay where method is typically debounce and delay is in milliseconds.

For values that change over time, you should use signals instead of regular properties. Regular property bindings like {{ welcomeMessage }} may not update automatically, but signal-based bindings like {{ theme() }} always track changes.

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <p>Your color preference is {{ theme() }}.</p>
  `,
})
export class App {
  theme = signal('dark');
}
```

When the user clicks a button that updates the signal to 'light', Angular automatically tracks the signal read in the template and updates the rendered page accordingly.

All expression values are converted to strings during text interpolation. Objects and arrays use their toString method for conversion.

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <p>User count: {{ users.length }}
    <p>Object info: {{ userInfo }}
  `,
})
export class App {
  users = ['Alice', 'Bob'];
  userInfo = { name: 'John', role: 'Admin' };
}
```

The rendered output will show:
- User count: 2
- Object info: [object Object]

For better object representation, consider using a custom pipe or signal-based value that returns a string.

Yes, you can use text interpolation anywhere you would normally write text in HTML. The double curly braces {{ }} tell Angular that it is responsible for the expression inside.

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    <p>Your color preference is {{ theme() }}.</p>
    <div>Current status: {{ status }}</div>
    <span>Array items: {{ items }}</span>
  `,
})
export class App {
  theme = signal('dark');
  status = 'active';
  items = [1, 2, 3];
}
```

Best practices:
1. Use signals for values that change over time to ensure automatic updates
2. All expressions are converted to strings automatically
3. Keep interpolation simple and avoid complex logic directly in templates

Yes, you can bind expressions including method calls directly to native properties. Angular's change detection mechanism monitors these expressions. Whenever isFormValid returns a different boolean value, the binding updates the disabled property on the HTMLButtonElement instance immediately.

```ts
// Component logic
title: string = 'Save';

isFormValid(): boolean {
  return this.form.valid;
}
```

This pattern allows you to control native element states declaratively.

Both approaches work but have different use cases:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-profile',
  template: `
    <ul [style]="listStyles()">
      <li>Item</li>
    </ul>
    <section [style]="sectionStyles()">
      <p>Content</p>
    </section>
  `,
})
export class UserProfile {
  // String approach - good for simple declarations
  listStyles = signal('display: flex; padding: 8px');

  // Object approach - better for complex styles with multiple properties
  sectionStyles = signal({
    border: '1px solid black',
    'font-weight': 'bold',
    color: '#333'
  });
}
```

Use strings when you need quick inline declarations. Use objects when managing multiple CSS properties or when you want TypeScript type safety for property names.

Angular uses the triple-equals operator (`===`) to compare previous and current values when binding style objects. You must create a new object instance for Angular to detect changes:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  template: `<div [style]="cardStyles()">Content</div>`,
})
export class CardComponent {
  cardStyles = signal({ color: 'blue' });

  updateColor() {
    // WRONG - same object reference, no update
    this.cardStyles.set({ color: 'red' });

    // CORRECT - new object instance required
    this.cardStyles.set({ ...this.cardStyles(), color: 'red' });
  }
}
```

Always spread the previous value and create a new object when modifying style objects to trigger Angular's change detection.

Chain @else if blocks with a final @else for the catch-all scenario:

```html
@if (order.status === 'pending') {
  <p>Your order is pending confirmation</p>
} @else if (order.status === 'shipped') {
  <p>Your order has been shipped and is on its way</p>
} @else if (order.status === 'delivered') {
  <p>Your order has been delivered successfully</p>
} @else {
  <p>Order status: {{ order.status }}</p>
}
```

Each condition evaluates sequentially until one matches, then the corresponding block renders.

Place @else if blocks immediately after their parent @if or previous @else if without line breaks between them. The @else block must be singular at the end:

```html
@if (user.role === 'admin') {
  <button (click)="deleteUser()">Delete User</button>
} @else if (user.role === 'editor') {
  <button (click)="editContent()">Edit Content</button>
} @else if (user.role === 'viewer') {
  <button (click)="viewOnly()">View Only</button>
} @else {
  <p>Access denied</p>
}
```

All structural directives must be on the same logical line sequence for proper template parsing.

You can use the `as` keyword to assign the conditional expression's result to a local variable for reuse within the block scope. This is ideal for long expressions that would otherwise clutter the template.

```html
@if (user.profile.settings.startDate; as startDate) {
  {{ startDate }}
}
```

The main benefit is improved readability and maintainability. Instead of repeating a complex expression or accessing properties multiple times, you can store the computed value once and reference it cleanly.

```html
@if (user.profile.settings.startDate; as startDate) {
  {{ startDate }}
}
```

Use the track by clause with a unique identifier from your collection. This tells Angular which item corresponds to which DOM element, preventing full list re-rendering on updates.

```html
@for (product of products; track product.id) {
  <div class="product-card">
    <h3>{{ product.name }}</h3>
    <p class="price">{{ product.price | currency }}</p>
  </div>
}
```

The `track product.id` clause ensures Angular only updates the specific DOM element for each product rather than re-rendering the entire list when data changes.

No, Angular's @for block does not support flow-modifying statements like continue or break. These JavaScript control flow statements are not available in Angular templates.

To achieve similar functionality, filter your collection before rendering:

```html
@for (product of products | filterActive; track product.id) {
  {{ product.name }}
}
```

Or use a conditional inside the block to skip specific items:

```html
@for (product of products; track product.id) {
  @if (product.active) {
    <div class="product-card">{{ product.name }}</div>
  }
}
```

This approach maintains Angular's rendering performance characteristics while giving you the control flow logic you need.

Angular has additional performance optimizations specifically for Array values. When your collection is a native JavaScript Array, Angular can leverage optimized iteration patterns.

For best performance:

```typescript
// Optimal - Native Array
products: Product[] = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Product A' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Product B' }
];

// Less optimal - Other iterables (Set, Map, etc.)
products: Set<Product> = new Set([
  { id: 1, name: 'Product A' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Product B' }
]);
```

Use native Arrays when possible for the @for block. If you must use other iterables, ensure they have stable unique identifiers in your track clause to minimize DOM operations during updates.

You should track by the unique id property, not the entire item object. Tracking by reference identity (track item) causes Angular to destroy and recreate DOM nodes on every change detection cycle since it cannot map which data corresponds to which node.

```html
@for (product of products; track product.id) {
  <div class="product-card">
    <h2>{{ product.name }}</h2>
    <p>{{ product.price | currency }}</p>
  </div>
}
```

When you use `track product.id`, Angular maintains a stable relationship between the data and DOM nodes. If only the price changes, Angular updates bindings without destroying the element. This reduces unnecessary change detection work and improves rendering performance significantly.

If your data lacks an id field, add one to your model:
```typescript
interface Product {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
}
```

Avoid `track product` unless you have no other option, as it forces reference-based tracking which is slower for dynamic collections.

Yes, you can use $index for static collections that never change order or content. This tells Angular to track each item by its position in the collection.

```html
@for (menu of menuItems; track $index) {
  <li [attr.data-index]="$index">
    {{ menu.title }}
  </li>
}
```

Limitations:
- Only works when items never change position or are removed/added
- If you insert, remove, or reorder items, Angular will incorrectly map DOM nodes to data
- Not suitable for dynamic lists where content changes frequently

Best use cases:
```typescript
// Static navigation menu
const staticMenu = ['Home', 'About', 'Contact'];

// Dashboard widgets with fixed positions
const dashboardWidgets = [WidgetA, WidgetB, WidgetC];
```

For dynamic collections where items may change, always prefer a unique property like id over $index to ensure correct view reuse and DOM stability.

When using `track item` (reference tracking), Angular compares object references using triple-equals (===). If the reference changes, Angular destroys and recreates the entire element.

When using `track item.id` (property tracking), Angular updates bindings without destroying elements if only properties change.

```html
<!-- Reference tracking - slower for property changes -->
@for (user of users; track user) {
  <div>
    <p>{{ user.name }}</p>
    <p>{{ user.email }}</p>
  </div>
}

<!-- Property tracking - optimized for property updates -->
@for (user of users; track user.id) {
  <div>
    <p>{{ user.name }}</p>
    <p>{{ user.email }}</p>
  </div>
}
```

Example scenario:
```typescript
// User object reference stays same, email updates
const user = { id: '1', name: 'John', email: 'john@example.com' };
user.email = 'newemail@example.com';

// With track user.id: Angular updates bindings only
// With track user: Angular may destroy/recreate element if reference changes
```

The @for block prioritizes view reuse unlike *ngFor. If a tracked property changes but object reference remains same, Angular updates the view's bindings (including component inputs) rather than destroying and recreating the entire element.

You can use the implicit $first and $last variables within your @for block. Here is the implementation:

```html
@for (item of items; track item.id; let isFirst = $first, isLast = $last) {
  <div class="item {{ isFirst ? 'start' : '' }} {{ isLast ? 'end' : '' }}">
    {{ item.name }}
  </div>
}
```

This allows you to apply specific CSS classes or logic based on the position of the current row in the collection.

You can use the let segment to create aliases for implicit variables like $index. This allows you to capture the value before it is shadowed by an inner block.

```html
@for (outer of outerItems; track outer.id; let idx = $index) {
  <div>Outer Index: {{ idx }}</div>
  @for (inner of innerItems; track inner.id) {
    <span>{{ inner.name }}</span>
  }
}
```

By aliasing $index to idx, the outer loop's index remains accessible even when nesting subsequent control flow blocks.

To handle empty states within control flow blocks, you can append an @empty block immediately after the closing brace of your @for loop. The content inside this block renders only when the iterable has no items.

```html
@for (item of items; track item.name) {
  <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>There are no items.</li>
}
```

This pattern ensures users see meaningful feedback without requiring additional @if wrappers around the list structure.

Yes, the @empty block must be placed immediately after the @for block's closing brace. Angular's control flow parser expects this structure to distinguish between the loop body and the empty state handler.

```html
@for (item of items; track item.name) {
  <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <p>No data available.</p>
}
```

Placing it on a new line or directly after the brace is valid, but it cannot be separated by other blocks like @if.

Angular's @switch block uses the triple-equals (===) operator for strict value comparison. Unlike JavaScript switch statements, @switch does not have a fallthrough behavior, so you do not need to add equivalent break or return statements within the block.

```html
@switch (userPermissions) {
  @case ('admin') {
    <app-admin-dashboard />
  }
}
```

You can specify multiple conditions for a single block by having consecutive @case statements. If no @case matches the expression and there is no @default block, nothing is shown.

```html
@switch (userPermissions) {
  @case ('reviewer')
  @case ('editor') {
    <app-editor-dashboard />
  }
  @default {
    <app-viewer-dashboard />
  }
}
```

Use @default never; to explicitly declare that no remaining cases should exist. If the union type is later extended and a new case is not covered by @case, Angular's template type checker will report an error.

```html
@Component({
  template: `
    @switch (state) {
      @case ('loggedOut') {
        <button>Login</button>
      }

      @case ('loggedIn') {
        <p>Welcome back!</p>
      }

      @default never;
    }
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  state: 'loggedOut' | 'loading' | 'loggedIn' = 'loggedOut';
}
```

This throws an error if @case ('loading') is missing because the union type includes 'loading' but no handler exists for it.

When the switched expression is nested within a union, you must explicitly specify the expression to check for exhaustiveness using @default never(expression);

```typescript
@Component({
  template: `
    @switch (state.mode) {
      @case ('show') { {{ state.menu }}; }
      @case ('hide') {}
      @default never(state);
    }
  `,
})
export class App {
  state!: {mode: 'hide'} | {mode: 'show'; menu: number};
}
```

The parameter passed to never() tells Angular which expression to verify exhaustiveness against when dealing with nested union types.

The @let syntax mirrors JavaScript's let statement but operates within the Angular template scope. It allows you to define a variable once at the top of the template and reference it multiple times without re-evaluation, improving performance and readability compared to repeating complex expressions.

```typescript
// app.component.ts
export class AppComponent {
  title = 'Angular App';
}
```

```html
<!-- app.component.html -->
@let greeting = 'Welcome to Angular';
<h1>{{ greeting }}</h1>
```

Use @let when a calculation or value is needed multiple times within the same view. This ensures the expression evaluates only once per change detection cycle rather than repeatedly, which optimizes rendering performance and reduces code duplication.

```typescript
// app.component.ts
export class AppComponent {
  get formattedPrice(): string {
    return '$' + this.price.toFixed(2);
  }
}
```

```html
<!-- app.component.html -->
@let price = 19.99;
<p>Cost: {{ price | currency }}</p>
```

You can directly reference the @let variable in any template context, including event handlers:

```html
@let user = user$ | async;

@if (user) {
  <button (click)="update(user)">Update profile</button>
}
```

The `user` variable declared with `@let` is scoped to the template block and can be passed as an argument to component methods, bound to inputs, or used in any Angular expression within that scope.

Yes, @let variables are accessible throughout the entire template block including nested control flows:

```html
@let user = user$ | async;

@if (user) {
  <ul>
    @for (snack of user.favoriteSnacks; track snack.id) {
      <li>{{ snack.name }}</li>
    }
  </ul>
}
```

The `user` variable remains available inside the `@for` block, allowing you to iterate over collections derived from the @let value. This pattern enables clean separation between data fetching and template rendering logic.

@let variables are scoped to their immediate template block. If you declare `@let user = user$ | async;` inside an `@if` block, that variable is not accessible outside of it:

```html
@if (user) {
  @let displayName = user.name;
  <h1>Hello, {{ displayName }}</h1>
}
<!-- displayName is NOT accessible here -->
<p>Cannot use displayName</p>
```

To share values across template blocks, either:
1. Declare the @let at a higher scope level
2. Pass the value as an input to child components
3. Use component methods that accept the data as parameters

To increment a counter, define a Signal in your component class and bind it to the template. The `@let` variable will reflect the current value of the signal without needing reassignment.

```typescript
count = signal(0);
```

```html
@let val = count();
<button (click)="count.update(c => c + 1)">Increment</button>
```

The @let keyword must be followed by one or more whitespaces but NOT including new lines. The JavaScript name can have zero or more whitespaces after it, and the expression following = can be multi-line. Here is the correct pattern:

@let result = 
  calculateTotal(items)
  .pipe(
    map(x => x * 1.1)
  );

Invalid syntax would place a newline between @let and the identifier. The semicolon ; must terminate every declaration regardless of expression complexity.

Yes, @let can reference other declarations or component properties. Here is a complete implementation:

<ng-container>
  @let basePrice = product.price;
  @let discountAmount = basePrice * (discount / 100);
  @let finalPrice = basePrice - discountAmount;

  <div class="price-card">
    <span>Original: ${{ basePrice }}</span>
    <span class="discount">-${{ discountAmount.toFixed(2) }}</span>
    <span class="final-price">${{ finalPrice.toFixed(2) }}</span>
  </div>
</ng-container>

Each declaration is independent and can reference previous @let values or component properties directly.

Yes, you can declare multiple template reference variables with unique names. Each variable maintains its own scope and reference to the specific element.

```html
<!-- Multiple reference variables -->
<input #taskInput placeholder="Enter task name" />
<input #priorityInput type="number" min="1" max="5" />
<select #statusSelect>
  <option value="pending">Pending</option>
  <option value="completed">Completed</option>
</select>
```

```typescript
// Access all references in component
@Component({
  selector: 'app-task-form',
  templateUrl: './task-form.component.html'
})
export class TaskFormComponent {
  onSubmit() {
    const task = this.taskInput.value;
    const priority = this.priorityInput.value;
    const status = this.statusSelect.value;

    console.log({ task, priority, status });
  }
}
```

Each variable (`#taskInput`, `#priorityInput`, `#statusSelect`) creates an independent reference to its respective element, enabling you to access their properties without using DOM queries or ViewChild decorators.

Yes, you can query children using @ViewChild with selector queries or template variable names. Here's the recommended pattern:

```html
<div #container>
  <input #description value="Original description" />
</div>
```

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `<div #container><input #description value="Original description">`,
})
export class AppComponent {
  @ViewChild('container') containerRef: ElementRef | undefined;

  @ViewChild('description') descriptionInput: ElementRef | undefined;

  // Access child element from parent
  getContainerValue(): string {
    return this.containerRef?.nativeElement.textContent || '';
  }

  // Direct child access via template variable
  getDescription(): string {
    return this.descriptionInput?.nativeElement.value || '';
  }
}
```

Best practices:
1. Always use ElementRef | undefined for type safety
2. Query after change detection runs (ngAfterViewInit lifecycle hook)
3. Use selector queries (@ContentChild, @ViewChild with { static: false }) when elements are dynamically added

The code for any components, directives, and pipes inside the `@defer` block is split into a separate JavaScript file. This file is loaded only when necessary, after the rest of the template has been rendered.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
}
```

This separation reduces the initial bundle size sent to the client.

The primary rule violated is the standalone requirement. Non-standalone dependencies cannot be deferred and are still eagerly loaded, even if placed inside @defer blocks. To fix this, ensure your component decorator explicitly includes `standalone: true`. 

Here is the correct configuration pattern:

@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-card',
  standalone: true,
  templateUrl: './user-card.component.html'
})
export class UserCardComponent {
}

Additionally, verify that this component is not referenced outside of the @defer block within the same file.

You utilize specific sub-blocks like load, visible, and error within the @defer structural directive to handle loading states gracefully. Here is a template example:

```html
<div @defer (load) {
  <app-content />
} error {
  <p>Loading failed</p>
}> 
</div>
```

This structure allows you to define fallback content for errors while deferring the main component until the load condition is met.

To handle idle states, you can use the idle sub-block alongside load and visible. This ensures content remains hidden if it hasn't been viewed yet after loading. Here is the implementation:

```html
<div @defer (load) {
  <app-data />
} idle {
  <div *ngIf="!data">Idle State</div>
}> 
</div>
```

This pattern gracefully manages the lifecycle by keeping the UI responsive while preventing unnecessary rendering of deferred content.

By default, `@defer` blocks do not render initially and do not block the main thread. The content is lazy-loaded and only renders once the browser state becomes idle. You can implement this pattern as follows:
```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
}
```
This ensures the heavy component loads after the initial page load completes.

You can specify custom triggers such as hover, click, or scroll to control when the deferred content loads. This allows you to optimize performance based on specific user actions:
```html
@defer on hover {
  <heavy-dashboard />
}
```
This configuration defers rendering until the user hovers over the element.

You can declare an optional `@placeholder` block within your `@defer` directive to show content before the trigger fires. Be aware that any dependencies in the placeholder section are eagerly loaded by Angular.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @placeholder {
  <p>Placeholder content</p>
}
```

To prevent fast flickering when deferred dependencies are fetched quickly, you can specify the `minimum` parameter in milliseconds or seconds. This ensures the placeholder remains visible after initial render.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @placeholder (minimum 500ms) {
  <p>Placeholder content</p>
}
```

Use the `after` and `minimum` parameters on your @loading block to control when the loading template appears:

```html
@defer (
  load after 100ms;
  minimum 1s
) {
  <large-component />
} @loading (after 100ms; minimum 1s) {
  <img alt="loading..." src="loading.gif" />
}
```

The `after` parameter specifies how long to wait after loading begins before showing the loading template. The `minimum` parameter ensures the placeholder is shown for at least that duration. Both parameters accept milliseconds (ms) or seconds (s). These timers start immediately when loading is triggered, preventing fast flickering when deferred dependencies fetch quickly.

The `@loading` block shows content while deferred dependencies are actively loading, whereas `@placeholder` displays static content that replaces both the loading state and deferred content once fully loaded.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @loading {
  <img alt="loading..." src="loading.gif" />
} @placeholder {
  <p>Placeholder content</p>
}
```

Key differences:
- `@loading` is optional and replaces `@placeholder` once loading triggers
- Both have eagerly loaded dependencies (similar to `@placeholder`)
- Use `@loading` when you want dynamic feedback during the fetch process
- Use `@placeholder` for static fallback content that persists until the deferred component fully loads
- You can use both together: `@loading` shows while fetching, then `@placeholder` shows as a final fallback if needed

To prevent fast flickering, configure both `after` and `minimum` parameters on your @loading block:

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @loading (after 100ms; minimum 1s) {
  <img alt="loading..." src="loading.gif" />
}
```

The `after 100ms` waits 100 milliseconds after loading begins before showing the loading template. The `minimum 1s` ensures the loading content stays visible for at least 1 second. Both timers begin immediately when loading is triggered, not when the component renders. This prevents users from seeing a flash of content that disappears too quickly, improving perceived performance.

To handle failures in deferred loading, you can use the optional @error block within your @defer directive. This block renders content specifically when the lazy-loaded dependencies fail to initialize. Note that unlike the main deferred content, the dependencies inside the @error block are eagerly loaded.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @error {
  <p>Failed to load large component.</p>
}
```

No, the dependencies of the @error block are eagerly loaded. This behavior is consistent with the @placeholder and @loading blocks. While the primary content waits for a load trigger or timeout, the error fallback resources are fetched immediately to ensure they are available if needed.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @error {
  <!-- Dependencies here are eager -->
}
```

The dependencies within the @error block are eagerly loaded, which is identical to how @placeholder and @loading blocks handle their dependencies. This ensures that error resources are ready immediately upon failure, unlike the main content which remains deferred.

```html
@defer {
  <large-component />
} @error {
  <!-- Eagerly loaded -->
}
```

Use the @defer directive with the when expression. Here is the implementation:

```html
@defer (when user.isPremium) {
  <premium-dashboard />
}
@placeholder {
  <div>Loading dashboard...</div>
}
```

This ensures the component loads only when the condition evaluates to true.

To configure prefetching on idle, use the `prefetch` keyword followed by `on idle`. The main trigger and prefetch trigger are separated with a semi-colon character (`;`). Here is the implementation:

```html
@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

This loads the JavaScript for `<large-cmp />` when the browser becomes idle, making resources available faster once the user interacts with the placeholder.

Yes, you can specify a custom idle timeout by adding milliseconds in parentheses after `on idle`. Here is the implementation with a 500ms timeout:

```html
@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle(500)) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

The prefetching will start after the browser remains idle for 500 milliseconds, balancing performance with resource loading timing.

A prefetch trigger enables advanced behaviors by loading JavaScript resources before the user has actually seen or interacted with the defer block. This makes resources available faster when the user does interact with it soon. The key benefit is proactive resource preparation:

```html
@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

Without prefetch, resources load only when the user interacts. With prefetch on idle, the browser starts loading in advance during idle periods, reducing perceived latency and improving user experience for components that may be interacted with shortly after becoming visible.

To manually step through states, set `DeferBlockBehavior.Manual` in the TestBed configuration. This prevents automatic playback and allows you to invoke specific states via the fixture API.

```typescript
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  deferBlockBehavior: DeferBlockBehavior.Manual
});
```

This setup ensures the component starts in a paused state, giving you full control over when to trigger rendering.

Retrieve the fixture via `getDeferBlocks()`, then call `render()` with the corresponding `DeferBlockState` enum value. Assert the DOM content after each transition to ensure correctness.

```typescript
const deferBlockFixture = (await componentFixture.getDeferBlocks())[0];
await deferBlockFixture.render(DeferBlockState.Loading);
expect(componentFixture.nativeElement.innerHTML).toContain('Loading');
await deferBlockFixture.render(DeferBlockState.Complete);
```

This sequence allows you to validate each rendering phase programmatically.

No, only standalone components support deferral. NgModule-based dependencies are included in the eagerly loaded bundle. Here is the correct pattern for a deferred standalone component versus an eager NgModule component.

```typescript
// Standalone Component (Deferred)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-deferred',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule],
  template: '<div>Standalone</div>'
})
export class DeferredComponent {}
```

```typescript
// NgModule Component (Eager)
@NgModule({
  declarations: [NgModuleComponent],
  exports: [NgModuleComponent]
})
export class MyModule {}
```

Dependencies inside an NgModule are not deferred. They load eagerly regardless of the `@defer` block usage on parent views. To utilize `@defer`, you must migrate components to standalone declarations.

```typescript
// Module Structure (Eager)
@NgModule({
  imports: [DeferredModule],
  exports: [NgModuleComponent]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

```typescript
// Standalone Component (Deferred)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-deferred',
  standalone: true,
  template: '<div>Standalone</div>'
})
export class DeferredComponent {}
```

No, deferring components visible in the user's viewport negatively affects Core Web Vitals by increasing Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS). You should prioritize eager loading for content immediately visible on the screen.

To implement this correctly, ensure visible components are imported directly rather than using lazy loading strategies that defer rendering:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';

// Eager Loading for Visible Components
@Component({
  selector: 'app-home',
  template: '<h1>Welcome</h1>'
})
class HomeComponent {}

@NgModule({
  declarations: [HomeComponent],
  imports: []
})
export class HomeModule {}
```

Avoid lazy loading configurations for these sections to prevent layout shifts during the initial page render.

If you must defer content, avoid `immediate`, `timer`, `viewport`, and custom `when` triggers that cause loading during the initial page render. These triggers can introduce delays that result in layout shifts.

Instead, configure your loading strategy to ensure immediate availability for visible components:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { loadComponent } from '@angular/platform-browser';

// Avoid 'viewport' or 'timer' triggers for visible content
@Component({
  selector: 'app-deferred',
  template: '<p>Deferred Content</p>'
})
class DeferredComponent {}

// Safe Loading Configuration
export const loadStrategy = {
  when: 'eager', // Do not use 'viewport' or 'timer'
  component: DeferredComponent
};
```

Ensure the `when` trigger is set to avoid delays that impact Core Web Vitals.

Screen readers that focus on a deferred section will initially read the placeholder or loading content. However, they may not announce changes when the deferred content loads. To ensure deferred content changes are announced to screen readers, you must wrap your @defer block in an element with a live region:

```html
<div aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
  @defer (on timer(2000)) {
    <user-profile [user]="currentUser" />
  } @placeholder {
    Loading user profile...
  } @loading {
    Please wait...
  } @error {
    Failed to load profile
  }
</div>
```

This ensures that changes are announced to the user when transitions (placeholder → loading → content/error) occur.

You should apply `aria-live="polite"` and `aria-atomic="true"` to the wrapper element. The `aria-live="polite"` attribute ensures that changes are announced without interrupting the user's current task, while `aria-atomic="true"` treats the entire content of the region as a single atomic unit for announcement purposes.

```html
<div aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
  @defer (on timer(2000)) {
    <user-profile [user]="currentUser" />
  }
</div>
```

Without these attributes, screen readers might miss the transition from placeholder to actual content.

Angular expressions treat `null` and `undefined` differently than standard JavaScript. In Angular, `null` is falsy but not equivalent to `undefined`. You should use the `?` operator for safe navigation or explicit checks.

```ts
// Angular Expression
{{ value | default:'fallback' }}

// Component Logic
if (value === null) {
  // Handle null specifically
}
```

Unlike standard JavaScript where truthy/falsy evaluation might vary, Angular expressions explicitly distinguish these states in template logic.

Angular expressions support standard boolean literals `true` and `false`. Unlike JavaScript where any non-zero number or object might be truthy, Angular expressions strictly evaluate these literals.

```ts
// Angular Expression
{{ isActive === true }}

// Component Logic
const isTrue = true;
```

When binding conditions, ensure you use explicit boolean values rather than relying on implicit truthiness to avoid runtime errors in the template.

You can use backticks with interpolation to build dynamic strings. Here is the pattern:
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-dynamic',
  // Dynamic class construction
  styles: [`.${className} { color: red; }`]
})
export class DynamicComponent {}
```

Tagged templates pass the string segments to a function. Syntax involves a tag identifier before the backticks:
```typescript
const result = tag`Hello ${name}`;
// Equivalent to:
tag('Hello ', name);
```

Angular supports standard object and array literals for configuration structures:
```typescript
const config = {
  apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com',
  items: ['Onion', 'Cheese', 'Garlic']
};
```

