// Component Method
parseValue(value: string): number {
  return parseInt(value);
}

// Template Usage
<div>{{ parseValue(input) }}</div>
```

Angular provides special local variables starting with `$` for loop contexts. In `@for` blocks, you can use `$index` to reference the current iteration count.

Example:
```ts
@for (item of items; track item) {
  <span>{{ $index }}</span>
}
```

Bitwise operators such as `&`, `~`, and `^=` are explicitly unsupported in this Angular environment. You must refactor logic to use standard arithmetic or boolean operators. For example, replace bitwise checks with logical conditions:

```typescript
// Unsupported
const result = a & b;

// Supported
const result = a && b;
```
Ensure all mathematical operations avoid bitwise syntax to maintain compatibility.

Object and array destructuring are unsupported in this strict configuration. You must access properties using direct bracket or dot notation. Refactor your initialization to assign values individually:

```typescript
// Unsupported
const { name } = person;

// Supported
const name = person.name;
```
Direct property assignment ensures the code adheres to the supported operator set.

Direct instantiation via the `new` keyword is restricted in this environment. Prefer Dependency Injection or factory functions for object creation. If you must create instances, use a service wrapper that handles the logic without direct `new` calls:

```typescript
// Unsupported
const car = new Car();

// Supported (via DI)
constructor(private carService: CarService) {}
```
Avoiding the `new` operator prevents compilation errors in this strict mode.

When a template variable shares the same name as a component member, it shadows that member. To unambiguously reference the class member, explicitly use 'this.' before the property name. Here is the pattern:

```html
<div>
  @let localData = someExpression();
  <!-- Accesses shadowed variable -->
  {{ localData }}
  <!-- Accesses original component property -->
  {{ this.data }}
</div>
```

For signal narrowing or any logic requiring explicit access to the component instance, prepend 'this.' to bypass lexical shadowing. This ensures you are referencing the component's signal rather than the local variable.

```html
<div>
  @let narrowed = this.mySignal();
  {{ narrowed }}
</div>
```

Event handlers are statements rather than expressions. Statements do not support pipes, which are designed for expression transformation.

```ts
// Invalid: Pipes not supported in statements
(click) => value | async

// Valid: Direct assignment
(click) => { value = newValue }
```

No, while statements support standard assignment operators, they do not support destructuring assignments. You must assign properties individually.

```ts
// Invalid: Destructuring not supported
(click) => ({ a, b } = obj)

// Valid: Standard property assignment
(click) => { obj.a = 1; obj.b = 2 }
```

Use the Angular CLI to generate the directive, then implement ElementRef injection to access the host DOM element and modify its style property:

```typescript
import {Directive, ElementRef, inject} from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]',
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  private el = inject(ElementRef);

  constructor() {
    this.el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';
  }
}
```

Apply the directive in your template:

```html
<p appHighlight>This text will have a yellow background</p>
```

The selector `[appHighlight]` creates an attribute-based directive that targets elements with this attribute. The constructor runs when Angular instantiates the directive, allowing you to modify the host element's styles.

Inject ElementRef from @angular/core and use its nativeElement property to access the host DOM element. For modern Angular versions, use the inject() function:

```typescript
import {Directive, ElementRef, inject} from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]',
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  private el = inject(ElementRef);

  constructor() {
    // Access the host element directly
    const hostElement = this.el.nativeElement;

    // Modify styles
    hostElement.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';

    // You can also use classList for class manipulation
    hostElement.classList.add('highlighted');
  }
}
```

Key points:
- ElementRef provides direct DOM access through nativeElement property
- The inject() function is the modern Angular DI approach
- Attribute selectors like `[appHighlight]` target elements with that attribute
- Directives execute their constructor when Angular creates them, giving you control over element manipulation at instantiation time

To detect user interactions such as mouseover or mouseout, you must use the `host` property within the `@Directive()` decorator. This allows you to subscribe to events of the DOM element that hosts the directive.

```typescript
@Directive({
  selector: '[highlight]'
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  constructor(private el: ElementRef) {}

  host = {
    'mouseenter': 'onMouseEnter()',
    'mouseleave': 'onMouseLeave()'
  };

  onMouseEnter() { this.highlight(); }
  onMouseLeave() { this.clearHighlight(); }

  highlight() { el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = '#f0f0f0'; }
}
```

The recommended pattern involves delegating style modifications to a dedicated helper method. This keeps your event handlers clean and ensures consistent styling logic across different events.

```typescript
export class HighlightDirective {
  constructor(private el: ElementRef) {}

  host = {
    'mouseenter': 'onMouseEnter()',
    'mouseleave': 'onMouseLeave()'
  };

  onMouseEnter() { this.highlight(); }
  onMouseLeave() { this.clearHighlight(); }

  highlight() {
    el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = 'rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.1)';
  }

  clearHighlight() {
    el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = '';
  }
}
```

