To make a directive structural, you need to inject both TemplateRef and ViewContainerRef from @angular/core. These services allow you to create embedded views at runtime.

```ts
import {Directive, TemplateRef, ViewContainerRef, inject} from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[myStructural]',
})
export class MyStructuralDirective {
  private templateRef = inject(TemplateRef);
  private viewContainerRef = inject(ViewContainerRef);
}
```

The CSS selector uses square brackets `[myStructural]` to indicate it's structural. When you create an embedded view with `viewContainerRef.createEmbeddedView(this.templateRef, context)`, Angular will render the template in place of the directive binding.

Use the `input()` function from @angular/core to define input properties, then fetch data in ngOnInit and pass it as context to createEmbeddedView. The `$implicit` key is used when you want the data to be accessible directly within the template.

```ts
import {Directive, TemplateRef, ViewContainerRef, inject, input} from '@angular/core';

export interface DataSource<T> {
  load(): Promise<T>;
}

@Directive({
  selector: '[select]',
})
export class SelectDirective {
  private templateRef = inject(TemplateRef);
  private viewContainerRef = inject(ViewContainerRef);

  selectFrom = input.required<DataSource<unknown>>();

  async ngOnInit() {
    const data = await this.selectFrom().load();
    this.viewContainerRef.createEmbeddedView(this.templateRef, {
      $implicit: data,
    });
  }
}
```

The template using this directive would be `<ng-template select><div>{{data}}</div></ng-template>` where `data` is available via `$implicit`.

