Configure the IMAGE_CONFIG provider in your app module or bootstrap file with the custom placeholderResolution value.

```ts
import { IMAGE_CONFIG } from '@angular-optimization/image';

@NgModule({
  providers: [
    {
      provide: IMAGE_CONFIG,
      useValue: {
        placeholderResolution: 40
      }
    }
  ]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

This changes the default generated placeholder size from 30px to 40px wide for all NgOptimizedImage components in your application. Note that if no image loader is provided, an error will be thrown when attempting to generate placeholders.

You can specify a base64 data URL directly in the ngSrc attribute without requiring an image loader. The format follows `data:image/[imagetype];base64,[encoded_data]` where [imagetype] is your image format like png, jpg, or webp.

```html
<img ngSrc="cat.jpg" width="400" height="200" placeholder="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0K..." />
```

You can generate the base64 encoding using JavaScript:

```javascript
const img = new Image();
img.src = 'cat.jpg';
img.onload = () => {
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = img.width;
  canvas.height = img.height;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  const base64Data = canvas.toDataURL('image/png');
  console.log(base64Data);
};
```

Or via command line with ImageMagick:

```bash
convert cat.jpg -quality 85 output.png && \
base64 -i output.png | tr -d '\n'
```

The Angular team recommends strict performance guidelines when using base64 placeholder images:

1. Keep placeholder images smaller than 4KB file size
2. Use them exclusively on critical images (above-the-fold content)
3. Decrease placeholder dimensions to reduce encoding size
4. Change image formats or parameters when saving images
5. At very low resolutions, compression parameters have a large effect on file size

```javascript
// Helper function to validate placeholder size before use
function isValidPlaceholderSize(base64Data) {
  const header = 'data:image/';
  const dataStart = base64Data.indexOf(header);
  if (dataStart === -1) return false;

  const imageData = base64Data.substring(dataStart + header.length);
  const byteLength = Math.ceil(imageData.length / 4 * 3);

  return byteLength < 4096; // 4KB limit
}

// Usage in component
const placeholder = isValidPlaceholderSize(placeholderData) ? 
  placeholderData : 'data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0K...';
```

For production, consider using a real image loader service instead of data URLs for non-critical images to maintain optimal bundle sizes.

You can encode images using JavaScript's canvas API or command line tools. Here are both approaches with size optimization:

```javascript
// Encode image to base64 with quality control
function encodeImageToBase64(file, maxWidth = 400, maxHeight = 200) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const reader = new FileReader();
    reader.onload = (e) => {
      const img = new Image();
      img.onload = () => {
        const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
        let width = img.width;
        let height = img.height;

        // Maintain aspect ratio
        if (width > maxWidth) {
          height = Math.round(height * maxWidth / width);
          width = maxWidth;
        } else if (height > maxHeight) {
          width = Math.round(width * maxHeight / height);
          height = maxHeight;
        }

        canvas.width = width;
        canvas.height = height;
        const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
        ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0, width, height);

        // Use JPEG for smaller file sizes
        const base64Data = canvas.toDataURL('image/jpeg', 0.75);
        resolve(base64Data);
      };
      img.onerror = reject;
      img.src = e.target.result;
    };
    reader.onerror = reject;
    reader.readAsDataURL(file);
  });
}
```

Command line encoding with ImageMagick:

```bash
# Convert to PNG with specific quality and dimensions
convert source.jpg -resize 400x200\! -quality 75 optimized.png && \
base64 -i optimized.png | tr -d '\n' > placeholder.txt
```

Key optimization parameters:
- Use JPEG format for photos (smaller than PNG)
- Set quality between 70-80 for placeholders
- Reduce dimensions to 400x200 or smaller
- Strip metadata before encoding

To fix distorted aspect ratios, add 'height: auto' to your CSS. This ensures the browser respects the intrinsic aspect ratio while allowing CSS to control dimensions.

```html
<img [src]="imageSrc" width="800" height="600" alt="Example" />
.example-img {
  height: auto;
  width: 100%;
}
```

This pattern prevents `NgOptimizedImage` from warning about distortion while maintaining responsive sizing.

Consider using fill mode and styling the parent element instead. This prevents attribute interference with CSS sizing.

```html
<div class="container">
  <img [src]="imageSrc" width="800" height="600" alt="Example" />
</div>
.container {
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 4/3;
}
```

This approach allows you to size the image via CSS without relying on HTML attributes that might conflict with your layout.

