To implement a custom loader for NgOptimizedImage, provide your loader function as a value for the IMAGE_LOADER DI token. The loader must accept ImageLoaderConfig and return an absolute URL:

```ts
providers: [
  {
    provide: IMAGE_LOADER,
    useValue: (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
      return `https://example.com/images?src=${config.src}&width=${config.width}&height=${config.height}`;
    },
  },
],
```

The loader function signature takes an object with the ImageLoaderConfig type from @angular/common as its argument and returns the absolute URL of the image asset. The config contains src (required), and optional width, height, and loaderParams properties.

A custom loader must use the width property to support requesting images at various widths in order for ngSrcset to work properly. Even though the width property may not always be present in your specific use case, omitting it will break srcset functionality:

```ts
providers: [
  {
    provide: IMAGE_LOADER,
    useValue: (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
      return `https://example.com/images?src=${config.src}&width=${config.width}&height=${config.height}`;
    },
  },
],
```

The width parameter is essential for NgOptimizedImage to generate proper srcset attributes that enable responsive image loading across different device pixel ratios and screen sizes.

The ImageLoaderConfig object contains the following properties that your custom loader must handle:

```ts
providers: [
  {
    provide: IMAGE_LOADER,
    useValue: (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
      return `https://example.com/images?src=${config.src}&width=${config.width}&height=${config.height}`;
    },
  },
],
```

Required properties:
- src: The source URL of the image asset (always present)

Optional properties:
- width: Image width parameter (must be used even if not always present for ngSrcset support)
- height: Image height parameter
- loaderParams: Additional loader-specific parameters

The loader function returns the absolute URL of the image asset by combining these configuration values into the final image request.

The `loaderParams` attribute accepts an object containing any properties you wish to inject into the loading process. This data is merged into the `ImageLoaderConfig` object and passed directly to your custom loader function, allowing full control over loader behavior without modifying the directive itself.

```typescript
// Component Template
<img [src]="" ngOptimizedImage="true" [loaderParams]="{ width: 800, quality: 95 }">

// Custom Loader Function
export const myLoader = (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
  console.log(config.loaderParams);
  return config.src;
};
```

You define the parameters in the template binding, and they become part of the `ImageLoaderConfig` object sent to your loader. This enables dynamic control over loading behavior, such as targeting specific CDN regions or output formats, by simply updating the object passed via `[loaderParams]`.

```typescript
// Component Template
<img [src]="" ngOptimizedImage="true" [loaderParams]="{ cdnRegion: 'us-east', format: 'webp' }">

// Loader Receiving Config
export const loader = (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
  const params = config.loaderParams;
  return `https://cdn.example.com/${params.cdnRegion}/image.${params.format}`;
};
```

Create a typed loader function that builds query parameters conditionally:

```ts
const myCustomLoader = (config: ImageLoaderConfig) => {
  let url = `https://example.com/images/${config.src}?`;
  let queryParams = [];
  if (config.width) {
    queryParams.push(`w=${config.width}`);
  }
  if (config.height) {
    queryParams.push(`h=${config.height}`);
  }
  if (config.loaderParams?.roundedCorners) {
    queryParams.push('mask=corners&corner-radius=5');
  }
  return url + queryParams.join('&');
};
```

Then use it in your template with the loaderParams binding:

```html
<img ngSrc="profile.jpg" width="300" height="300" [loaderParams]="{roundedCorners: true}" />
```

The loaderParams object allows you to control custom CDN features without modifying the base URL structure.

