`linkedSignal` tracks dependencies through the computation function you provide. When any signal accessed within that function changes, `linkedSignal` re-evaluates and updates to the new result.

Execution flow example:
```typescript
const shippingOptions = signal(['Ground', 'Air', 'Sea']);
const selectedOption = linkedSignal(() => shippingOptions()[0]);

console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Ground'

// Direct set on linkedSignal overrides computation temporarily
selectedOption.set(shippingOptions()[2]);
console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Sea'

// When shippingOptions changes, linkedSignal recomputes
shippingOptions.set(['Email', 'Will Call', 'Postal service']);
console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Email' - automatically updated
```

The computation function acts as a dependency tracker. Angular's signal system monitors which signals are accessed during the function execution and triggers re-computation when those dependencies change.

Calling `set()` on a `linkedSignal` temporarily overrides the computed value, but the dependency link remains intact. The next time the linked state changes, the signal will recompute and restore alignment with the source.

Demonstration:
```typescript
const shippingOptions = signal(['Ground', 'Air', 'Sea']);
const selectedOption = linkedSignal(() => shippingOptions()[0]);

console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Ground'

// Manual override - breaks sync temporarily
selectedOption.set(shippingOptions()[2]);
console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Sea'

// When source changes, link restores automatically
shippingOptions.set(['Email', 'Will Call', 'Postal service']);
console.log(selectedOption()); // 'Email' - recomputed from linked state
```

