Template-driven forms are ideal for small or simple forms where quick development and straightforward two-way data binding are priorities. They allow you to use form-specific directives in your Angular template without managing complex state objects manually. Reactive forms are better for scalable, complex forms requiring dynamic control over validation logic and model updates.

```typescript
// Example: Simple Template-Driven Form Component
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-login-form',
  templateUrl: './login-form.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./login-form.component.css'],
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FormsModule]
})
export class LoginFormComponent {
  loginForm = {
    username: '',
    password: ''
  };
}
```

```html
<!-- Example: Template-Driven Form HTML -->
<form #loginForm="ngForm" (ngSubmit)="onSubmit()">
  <input 
    type="text" 
    [(ngModel)]="loginForm.username" 
    name="username"
    required>

  <input 
    type="password" 
    [(ngModel)]="loginForm.password" 
    name="password"
    required>

  <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>
```

This pattern binds the control elements directly to data properties in your component class, updating the model as changes are made in the template.

To enable two-way data binding in Angular forms, you must import FormsModule into your module and use the [(ngModel)] syntax in your component template. This binds the element's value directly to a property on your component class.

