The NgModel directive automatically applies these classes based on user interaction. The ng-touched class appears when the control has been visited, while ng-untouched is present initially.

The dirty state indicates the control's value has changed from its initial value, while pristine means it hasn't been modified. Use these classes in your styling:

<form [formGroup="myForm"]>
  <input formControlName="username" class="control-input">
</form>

<style>
  .control-input.ng-pristine {
    background-color: #fff;
  }

  .control-input.ng-dirty {
    background-color: #f0f8ff;
  }

  .control-input.ng-dirty:not(.ng-valid) {
    box-shadow: 0 0 5px rgba(217, 83, 79, 0.5);
  }
</style>

The ng-dirty class is applied when the control value changes from its initial state. The ng-pristine class remains until modification occurs.

The ng-submitted class is applied to form elements upon submission, but not to controls inside the form. This means you track form-level submission state separately from control-level states:

<form [formGroup="myForm"] (ngSubmit)="onSubmit()" class="form-container">
  <input formControlName="email" class="control-input">
</form>

<style>
  .form-container.ng-submitted {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .control-input.ng-touched.ng-invalid {
    border-color: #d9534f;
  }
</style>

Important: You cannot apply ng-submitted to individual controls within the form. Use it on the container form element to detect when the entire form has been submitted.

To create visual feedback for form validation states, you need to define CSS classes for the ng-* attributes that Angular automatically applies. Create a forms.css file with these class definitions:

forms.css

ng-valid {
  border-left: 4px solid #28a745;
}

ng-invalid {
  border-left: 4px solid #dc3545;
}

index.html (styles)

<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/forms.css">
</head>

When you bind form controls to input elements, Angular automatically adds these classes based on validation state. The colored bar on the left provides immediate visual feedback when users interact with invalid fields.

To mark required fields and show invalid data at the same time, combine CSS classes targeting both validation states and required field indicators:

forms.css

.ng-invalid .required-field {
  border-left: 4px solid #dc3545;
}

.required-field::before {
  content: '*';
  color: #dc3545;
  margin-right: 4px;
}

index.html (styles)

<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/forms.css">
</head>

In your template, add the required attribute to form controls:

<input type="text" name="username" required ngModel>

Angular will apply ng-invalid when validation fails and the required-field class styling will show both the colored border indicator and asterisk marker.

Organize your Angular project with forms.css as a sibling to index.html for optimal style loading:

Project Structure:
adev/src/content/examples/forms/
  ├── src/
