To dynamically manage validators, use the `addValidators` method on the `AbstractControl` instance. This allows you to append validation logic without recreating the control or losing its current state.

```typescript
import { FormControl } from '@angular/forms';

const emailControl = new FormControl('');

// Define a custom validator function
function customEmailValidator(c: AbstractControl) {
  return null; // Valid if no error
}

// Add the validator dynamically based on state
if (someCondition) {
  emailControl.addValidators(customEmailValidator);
}
```

Alternatively, use `setValidators` to replace all existing validators with a new set.

When a field transitions from required to optional, you should remove the specific validation function using `removeValidators`. This ensures the control retains its value and other validations while clearing only the unwanted rule.

```typescript
import { AbstractControl } from '@angular/forms';

const emailControl: AbstractControl = this.form.get('email');

// Remove a specific validator reference
if (isOptional) {
  emailControl.removeValidators(this.requiredValidator);
}
```

Avoid recreating the control instance, as that resets internal state and breaks form binding.

You can use the `addValidators` and `removeValidators` methods on the AbstractControl instance to update rules. Crucially, you must call `updateValueAndValidity()` immediately after modification to recalculate the control's validity state.

```ts
onCountryChange(country: string) {
  const postalCodeControl = this.profileForm.get('postalCode');

  if (country === 'US') {
    postalCodeControl.addValidators([
      Validators.required,
      Validators.pattern(/^\d{5}$/)
    ]);
  } else {
    postalCodeControl.removeValidators([Validators.required]);
  }

  postalCodeControl.updateValueAndValidity();
}
```

Yes, you can pass an array of validator functions directly to `addValidators` to apply multiple rules in a single call. This is more efficient than calling the method individually for each validator.

```ts
// Apply multiple validators simultaneously
control.addValidators([
  Validators.required,
  Validators.minLength(8),
  Validators.pattern('^[a-zA-Z0-9]+$')
]);

// Ensure state updates after modification
control.updateValueAndValidity();
```

To replace all existing synchronous validators on an AbstractControl, use the `setValidators` method with an array of validation functions. If you wish to remove all validators, use `clearValidators`. After modifying the validator list, you must call `updateValueAndValidity()` to trigger re-evaluation.

```ts
toggleStrictNameValidation(isStrict: boolean) {
  const nameControl = this.profileForm.get('name');

  if (isStrict) {
    // Set strict validation rules
    nameControl.setValidators([
      Validators.required,
      Validators.minLength(3),
      Validators.pattern(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/),
    ]);
  } else {
    // Clear all validators
    nameControl.clearValidators();
  }

  nameControl.updateValueAndValidity();
}
```

For asynchronous validation logic, Angular provides specific methods distinct from synchronous ones. Use `setAsyncValidators` to replace all existing async validators or `clearAsyncValidators` to remove them entirely. You can also add or remove individual async validators using `addAsyncValidators` and `removeAsyncValidators`. The pattern for updating validity remains the same.

```ts
// Replace async validators
control.setAsyncValidators([
  Validators.asyncValidator1,
  Validators.asyncValidator2,
]);

// Clear all async validators
control.clearAsyncValidators();

// Trigger validation update
control.updateValueAndValidity();
```

Changing the validator list modifies the control's configuration but does not automatically re-run the validation logic. Calling `updateValueAndValidity()` forces Angular to evaluate the current value against the new set of validators and update the control's status (valid/invalid) accordingly. This ensures the UI reflects the correct state immediately after dynamic changes.

```ts
// Always call this after modifying validators
control.setValidators([...]);
control.updateValueAndValidity(); // Essential for immediate feedback
```

To prevent propagation and event emission, pass an options object with `onlySelf: true` and `emitEvent: false`. This isolates the update to the specific control.

```ts
control.updateValueAndValidity({
  onlySelf: true,
  emitEvent: false
});
```

This pattern is useful when updating internal logic without triggering UI updates or parent state changes.

Define a FormMetadata interface that captures control properties like label, type, and validation rules. This allows the component to iterate over metadata and instantiate FormControl instances dynamically.

```typescript
interface FormField {
  label: string;
  type: 'text' | 'number' | 'email';
  required: boolean;
  placeholder?: string;
}

interface FormConfig {
  fields: FormField[];
  submitLabel: string;
}
```

This metadata-driven approach decouples the UI structure from the business logic, enabling rapid updates to the form without code changes.

