Hydration restores the application by attaching event listeners to existing DOM nodes rather than creating new ones. This preserves the server-rendered HTML structure while enabling client-side interactivity.

Here is a conceptual example of how state persistence aligns with this restoration:

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: '<div *ngIf="data">{{ data }}</div>'
})
export class AppComponent {
  constructor(private transferState: TransferState) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    // Hydration reuses the existing DOM structure
    this.data = this.transferState.get('initialData', null);
  }
}

To transfer data efficiently, Angular uses `provideServerTransferState` to persist state from the server response to the client. This avoids re-fetching data on the client.

Implementation pattern:

import { provideServerTransferState } from '@angular/ssr';
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';

const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideServerTransferState()
  ]
};

// Server-side setup
export function renderComponent() {
  const state = new TransferState();
  // Persist data retrieved by server
  state.set('userProfile', JSON.stringify(userProfile));
  return html + state.serialize();
}

