In Angular v18, you can resolve pre-hydration interaction issues by enabling the Event Replay feature. This captures events occurring before hydration completes and replays them once the client-side listeners are attached. You need to configure `provideClientHydration` with the `withEventReplay()` function in your application bootstrap provider.

```typescript
import { provideClientHydration, withEventReplay } from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration(withEventReplay())],
});
```

This setup ensures that any user actions performed before the JavaScript environment is fully initialized are queued and executed immediately after hydration finishes.

To enable event replaying, you must import `provideClientHydration` and `withEventReplay` from '@angular/platform-browser'. Then, pass `withEventReplay()` as an argument to `provideClientHydration()` within the providers array of your `bootstrapApplication` configuration.

```typescript
import { provideClientHydration, withEventReplay } from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration(withEventReplay())],
});
```

Ensure that the `withEventReplay()` function is nested directly inside `provideClientHydration` to activate the feature correctly for your application.

Event Replay automatically handles this by capturing native browser events before hydration completes. The system stores them in the Event Contract in-memory, then relaunches them once hydration finishes. No additional configuration is needed for standard cases:

```typescript
// Angular SSR app - Event Replay works out of the box
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent);
```

The three-phase process handles this automatically:
1. **Capturing**: All native events (click, mouseover, focusin) are recorded before hydration
2. **Storing**: Event Contract maintains them in memory without loss
3. **Relaunching**: Events re-trigger after hydration completes

If you have incremental hydration enabled, event replay is automatically activated under the hood.

Event Replay supports all native browser events including click, mouseover, focusin, and other standard interactions. The underlying JSAction library handles the event dispatch mechanism:

```typescript
// Native events automatically supported
const events = ['click', 'mouseover', 'focusin', 'keydown', 'keyup'];

// Event Contract stores these in-memory during pre-hydration phase
// No custom configuration needed for standard native events
```

For advanced scenarios, you can read the JSAction library documentation at https://github.com/angular/angular/tree/main/packages/core/primitives/event-dispatch#readme to understand how event dispatch works under the hood. The Event Contract keeps all recorded interactions in memory ensuring none are lost during the hydration window.

Event Replay functionality depends on the hydration process completing. If you have incremental hydration enabled, event replay is automatically activated under the hood and handles pre-hydration interactions seamlessly.

```typescript
// With incremental hydration enabled - Event Replay auto-activated
const appConfig = {
  incrementalHydration: true,
  // Event Replay is automatically enabled here
};
```

If you disable incremental hydration, you need to ensure your hydration process completes before critical user interactions occur. The three-phase system (capturing, storing via Event Contract, relaunching) only functions properly when Angular can complete the hydration phase to trigger event relaunch.

The primary culprit is direct DOM manipulation using native APIs after the initial render. Hydration expects the DOM tree structure to remain exactly as generated by the server-side rendering process. Any modification via document.querySelector or similar methods breaks this contract.

To resolve this, use Angular lifecycle hooks instead of native DOM access:

```typescript
import { Component, AfterViewInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: '<div>Content</div>'
})
export class ExampleComponent implements AfterViewInit {
  ngAfterViewInit() {
    // Safe to access Angular elements here
    const el = this.el.nativeElement;
    console.log('View initialized');
  }
}
```

Avoid modifying the DOM directly in ngOnInit or via event listeners that alter structure before hydration completes.

Using native DOM APIs like innerHTML bypasses Angular's change detection and hydration reconciliation, causing mismatches between the server-rendered HTML and client-side DOM structure.

To fix this, refactor your component to use Angular APIs. If you need dynamic content injection, prefer using template bindings or Renderer2 instead of direct manipulation.

Example refactoring:

// Avoid (Causes Hydration Error)
this.elementRef.nativeElement.innerHTML = content;

// Recommended (Angular API)
import { Renderer2, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
constructor(private renderer: Renderer2, private el: ElementRef) {}

ngAfterViewInit() {
  this.renderer.setProperty(this.el.nativeElement, 'innerHTML', content);
}

If refactoring is not immediately possible, you can use the ngSkipHydration attribute to bypass hydration for that specific component:

<div [ngSkipHydration]="true">Content</div>

Note: Skipping hydration disables SSR benefits for that component.

Yes, you can isolate the hydration behavior for specific components using the ngSkipHydration attribute. This is useful when refactoring legacy code to Angular APIs is not feasible immediately.

Apply the attribute directly to the host element of the component in your template:

<app-legacy-component [ngSkipHydration]="true"></app-legacy-component>

Alternatively, if you are using standalone components, you can pass it via input binding or set it on the host directive.

Example implementation:

@Component({
  selector: 'app-legacy-component',
  template: '<div>Legacy Content</div>',
  host: {
    '[ngSkipHydration]': 'true'
  }
})
export class LegacyComponent {}

Warning: Skipping hydration means the component will not benefit from Server-Side Rendering optimizations, potentially impacting initial load performance and SEO for that specific section.

