  selector: 'app-example',
  host: {ngSkipHydration: 'true'},
})
class ExampleComponent {}
```

Note that this forces the component to destroy and re-render itself, bypassing hydration benefits for this subtree.

This error indicates your application failed to reach a stable state within the timeout window before Angular attempted hydration. Stability is required because hydration and post-hydration processes only occur once stability is reported. Common causes include unresolved promises, pending microtasks, or excessive timeouts/interval usage during initialization.

To resolve this, refactor your component initialization to avoid blocking the stability check:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: '<div>Hello</div>'
})
export class AppComponent {
  // Avoid async operations that delay stability reporting in ngOnInit
  constructor() {
    // Do not use setTimeout or intervals here
  }
}
```

Ensure all critical data loads are handled outside the initial stability check window.

Pending microtasks and unresolved promises can delay the stability report, triggering the NG0506 error. You should refactor initialization logic to ensure no heavy async work blocks the stability check.

Here is a pattern for handling data without impacting stability:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-data',
  template: '<div>{{ data }}</div>'
})
export class DataComponent {
  data: any;

  ngOnInit() {
    // Use async pipe in template instead of blocking microtasks here
    this.data = new Observable(observer => {
      observer.next('Loaded');
      observer.complete();
    });
  }
}
```

By moving heavy logic to the template or deferring it, you allow Angular to report stability immediately.

Use the `provideStabilityDebugging` utility in your application providers. This feature logs pending tasks to the console when stabilization takes longer than expected.

```typescript
import { provideStabilityDebugging } from '@angular/core';
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideStabilityDebugging()
  ]
});
```

This utility is provided by default in dev mode when using `provideClientHydration`, but you can add it manually for production bundles or SSR without hydration scenarios. The feature will log information to the console if your application takes longer than expected to stabilize.

Import the `zone.js/plugins/task-tracking` plugin alongside your stability debugging provider. This combination provides stack traces of macrotask creation, helping you pinpoint the source of delays.

```typescript
import { provideStabilityDebugging } from '@angular/core';
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import 'zone.js/plugins/task-tracking'; // Use if you have Zone.js with provideZoneChangeDetection

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideStabilityDebugging()
  ]
});
```

When enabled, the utility logs pending tasks (`PendingTasks`) to the console. The task-tracking plugin shows which macrotasks are keeping the Angular Zone from stabilizing and provides the stack trace of macrotask creation.

Yes, but with important caveats. The `provideStabilityDebugging` utility is NOT removed from production bundles by Angular. You must manually exclude it for optimized production builds.

```typescript
import { provideStabilityDebugging } from '@angular/core';
