To prevent Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), you must ensure the DOM structure remains identical during server-side rendering and hydration. Avoid using @if blocks that conditionally render content based on platform checks like isPlatformBrowser, as this causes layout shifts when the browser takes over.

Instead, render a consistent placeholder or the final state in the template for both SSR and CSR. Handle the loading logic via CSS classes or component lifecycle hooks without altering the DOM height or width during hydration.

Example implementation:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { isPlatformBrowser } from '@angular/common';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  template: `
    <div class="dashboard-container">
      <!-- Consistent structure for SSR and CSR -->
      <div *ngIf="isLoading" class="loading-spinner"></div>
      <div *ngIf="!isLoading" class="content-wrapper">
        <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  `,
  styles: [`
    .dashboard-container {
      min-height: 200px; /* Prevents layout shift */
    }
    .loading-spinner {
      display: block;
    }
    .content-wrapper {
      display: none; /* Hidden initially, shown when data loads */
    }
  `]
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  isLoading = true;

  constructor(private platformId: any) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    // Logic to set loading state without changing DOM structure
    this.isLoading = false;
  }
}
```

Note: While the example above uses *ngIf, ensure the CSS classes handle visibility transitions smoothly (e.g., opacity fade) rather than appearing/disappearing abruptly. Ideally, avoid structural directives that change content entirely if possible, preferring state-driven visibility.

Platform checks like isPlatformBrowser should be used for logic or event binding, not for rendering content that affects layout. If the content must differ based on platform (e.g., showing a server error message vs client data), ensure the DOM slot exists in both SSR and CSR to prevent shifts.

Use Angular's hydration attributes or CSS-based visibility toggles instead of structural directives that remove elements from the DOM tree.

Example pattern:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { isPlatformBrowser } from '@angular/common';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-feature',
  template: `
    <div class="feature-container">
      <!-- Always present in DOM for SSR consistency -->
      <div [class.hidden]="isServer" class="content-slot"></div>
    </div>
  `,
  styles: [`
    .feature-container {
      min-height: 100px;
    }
    .hidden {
      display: none;
    }
  ]
})
export class FeatureComponent {
  isServer = true;

  constructor(private platformId: any) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    // Safe platform check for logic, not rendering
    if (!isPlatformBrowser(this.platformId)) {
      this.isServer = true;
    } else {
      this.isServer = false;
      // Fetch data here
    }
  }
}
```

Key takeaway: Keep the HTML structure static. Use CSS classes to toggle visibility rather than removing elements from the DOM tree.

Third-party scripts modify the DOM before hydration occurs, causing a mismatch between the server-rendered HTML and the client-side DOM structure expected by Angular. To resolve this, defer script execution until after hydration using the `afterNextRender` function.

```typescript
import { afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';

afterNextRender(() => {
  // Load or execute third-party script here
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = 'https://example.com/analytics.js';
  document.head.appendChild(script);
});
```

Use the `afterNextRender` API from Angular Core to schedule script injection after the initial render and hydration phase. This ensures the DOM matches Angular's expectations before external modifications occur.

```typescript
import { afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';

export function deferAnalytics() {
  const analyticsScript = document.createElement('script');
  analyticsScript.src = 'https://cdn.example.com/analytics.js';

  afterNextRender(() => {
    document.head.appendChild(analyticsScript);
  });
}
```

To configure incremental hydration, you must enable the server-side rendering feature in your application configuration. This allows Angular to manage hydration timing granularly. Here is the standard setup for app.config.ts:

import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideServerHydration } from '@angular/ssr';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideServerHydration({
      // Configuration options for hydration timing control
    })
  ]
};

This configuration initializes the necessary environment for the framework to manage hydration timing incrementally rather than globally.

Standard hydration processes the entire application DOM immediately upon loading, whereas Incremental Hydration leaves specific sections dehydrated. This reduces the initial JavaScript execution and DOM manipulation overhead by triggering hydration only when those sections are accessed.

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';

const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'heavy-section',
    loadChildren: () => import('./heavy/heavy.module').then(m => m.HeavyModule)
  }
];

@NgModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes)]
})
export class AppRoutingModule {}
```

Hydration is triggered incrementally when the user navigates to or interacts with a specific component that was initially left in a dehydrated state. The framework detects the need to render the client-side logic and hydrates the server-rendered HTML for that specific module.

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-interactable-section',
  template: '<button (click)="handleClick()">Interact</button>'
})
export class InteractableSectionComponent {
  handleClick(): void {
    console.log('Hydration triggered');
  }
}
```

Incremental hydration is enabled by default when you use `provideClientHydration()`. You do not need additional configuration. However, note that it automatically enables event replay. If you already have `withEventReplay()` in your providers list, you can safely remove it.

```ts
import {bootstrapApplication, provideClientHydration} from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration()],
});
```

To opt out of incremental hydration, use `withNoIncrementalHydration()` within the `provideClientHydration` provider configuration.

```ts
import {
  bootstrapApplication,
  provideClientHydration,
  withNoIncrementalHydration,
} from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration(withNoIncrementalHydration())],
});
```

To implement incremental hydration boundaries, add a `hydrate` trigger to your @defer block. This tells Angular to load dependencies during server-side rendering and render the main template instead of the placeholder.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  template: `
    <div [@defer]">
      <button (click)="loadData()">Load Data</button>
      <ng-content></ng-content>
    </div>
  `,
  hostDirectives: [
    DeferBlockComponent
  ]
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  @defer on hydrate = loadDependencies();

  loadData() {
    // Fetch data when user interacts
  }
}
```

The key pattern is using `@defer` with the `hydrate` trigger. During SSR, Angular loads dependencies and renders the main template. On client-side, dependencies remain deferred until the hydrate trigger fires.

Browser events triggered by users prior to hydration are automatically queued and replayed once the hydration process completes. Angular captures event listeners registered in your component and replays them after the defer block hydrates.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-form',
  template: `
    <form [defer]="on hydrate = loadForm()">
      <input type="text" (keyup)="handleKeyup($event)" />
      <button (click)="submitForm()">Submit</button>
    </form>
  `,
  hostDirectives: [
    DeferBlockComponent
  ]
})
export class FormComponent {
  @defer on hydrate = loadForm();

  handleKeyup(event: KeyboardEvent) {
    // This will be queued and replayed after hydration
    console.log('Key pressed:', event.key);
  }

  submitForm() {
    // Form submission queued until hydration complete
  }
}
```

Angular's event replay mechanism automatically captures listeners registered in your component. Events like keyup, click, and other user interactions are stored in a queue during the dehydrated state and executed once the hydrate trigger fires.

Incremental hydration extends full-application hydration by adding granular control through @defer blocks with hydrate triggers. Here's the key difference:

```typescript
// Full application hydration - entire app hydrated at once
@ApplicationConfig({
  hydration: {
    mode: 'full'
  }
})

// Incremental hydration - specific boundaries via @defer
@Component({
  template: `
    <!-- SSR renders main template, client defers until hydrate -->
    <div [@defer] on hydrate = loadHeavyComponent()">
      <heavy-component></heavy-component>
    </div>
  `,
  hostDirectives: [DeferBlockComponent]
})
```

During SSR with incremental hydration:
- Dependencies are loaded during server-side rendering
- Main template renders instead of @placeholder
- Content stays dehydrated on client until hydrate trigger fires

On client-side rendering:
- Dependencies remain deferred
- Defer block content stays dehydrated until hydrate trigger fires
- Event listeners are queued and replayed after hydration completes

This provides better performance by loading heavy components only when needed while maintaining event consistency.

For content that should load when entering the viewport, use `hydrate on viewport` with the Intersection Observer API. This ensures the large component only loads when visible, improving initial page performance.

```html
@defer (hydrate on viewport) {
  <dashboard-large-component />
} @placeholder {
  <div class="skeleton" style="height: 400px; background: #f0f0f0;"></div>
}
```

The browser will automatically observe when the component enters the viewport and trigger hydration at that moment. You can also add a fallback placeholder to maintain layout stability during loading.

Use `hydrate on idle` with an optional timeout parameter passed to requestIdleCallback. This ensures your heavy component loads when the browser has spare capacity, but won't wait indefinitely.

```html
@defer (hydrate on idle(500)) {
  <analytics-tracker />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Analytics loading...</div>
}
```

The `500` parameter specifies a 500ms timeout. If the browser doesn't schedule the callback within that window, the work executes immediately. You can adjust this value based on your performance requirements - typical values range from 100ms to 2000ms depending on expected idle periods.

`hydrate on immediate` loads deferred content as soon as all non-deferred content finishes rendering - essentially after the initial DOM is ready. `hydrate on timer` waits for a specific duration before loading.

```html
<!-- Immediate: loads after initial render completes -->
@defer (hydrate on immediate) {
  <heavy-component />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Loading...</div>
}

<!-- Timer: delays by specified duration regardless of render state -->
@defer (hydrate on timer(1s)) {
  <heavy-component />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Loading...</div>
}
```

Use `immediate` when you want the component to load as soon as possible after initial content is ready. Use `timer` when you need to delay loading for a specific period - useful for analytics, ads, or features that don't impact core user experience but should eventually load.

To implement conditional hydration, you can use the `hydrate when` trigger within the `@defer` directive. This allows Angular to load dependencies and hydrate content only when your condition evaluates to truthy. Here is the standard implementation pattern:

```html
@defer (hydrate when condition) {
  <large-cmp />
}
@placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

Ensure that the `condition` expression is resolvable by Angular at runtime.

With `hydrate never`, dependencies remain dehydrated on the initial server-side render to save bandwidth. However, once the application is running on the client side, dependencies will load based on viewport visibility rather than remaining static.

```html
@defer (on viewport; hydrate never) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

In this example, subsequent client-side renders would load the `@defer` block dependencies on viewport.

