```html
@defer (hydrate on interaction) {
  <parent-block-cmp />
  @defer (hydrate on hover) {
    <child-block-cmp />
  } @placeholder {
    <div>Child placeholder</div>
  }
} @placeholder {
  <div>Parent Placeholder</div>
}
```

In this example, hovering triggers the parent hydration first (<parent-block-cmp />), followed immediately by the child hydration (<child-block-cmp />). This ensures that dependencies are resolved before rendering dependent components.

Yes, you can configure different interaction triggers for each nested level independently. Each @defer block accepts its own trigger strategy definition.

```html
@defer (hydrate on interaction) {
  <parent-block-cmp />
  @defer (hydrate on hover) {
    <child-block-cmp />
  } @placeholder {
    <div>Child placeholder</div>
  }
} @placeholder {
  <div>Parent Placeholder</div>
}
```

Here, the parent uses `on interaction` while the child uses `on hover`. The system respects these individual configurations while maintaining the required top-down execution order during hydration.

