This configuration runs every 1000 milliseconds to check if any bindings were updated without a zoneless change detection notification. Angular will throw `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` if it detects an updated binding that would not have refreshed by the zoneless change detection cycle.

Angular throws `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` when a binding is updated without proper notification in zoneless mode. This indicates your application is making updates to state in a way that is not zoneless-compatible.

To debug this, enable the exhaustive check mode with an appropriate interval:

```typescript
import { provideCheckNoChangesConfig } from '@angular/core';

export function setupZonelessDebugging() {
  return () => {
    provideCheckNoChangesConfig({
      exhaustive: true,
      interval: 500
    });
  };
}
```

The error message will point to the specific binding that was updated without notification. Use this information to identify where your state management logic needs adjustment for zoneless compatibility.

The `interval` parameter specifies how frequently (in milliseconds) Angular checks for unnotified binding updates. Lower values increase detection sensitivity but add overhead; higher values reduce performance impact but may miss transient issues.

Recommended configurations:

```typescript
// Development - high sensitivity
provideCheckNoChangesConfig({
  exhaustive: true,
  interval: 100
});

// Production - balanced approach
provideCheckNoChangesConfig({
  exhaustive: false,
  interval: 500
});
```

For production, disable exhaustive mode to reduce runtime overhead. The check is primarily useful during development and testing phases to catch zoneless compatibility issues before deployment.

