HttpClient automatically strips the XSSI prefix (typically `)]}',\n`) when parsing JSON from HTTP responses, so you do not need to manually strip it. The framework handles this transparently during response processing.

```typescript
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

getApiData(): Promise<any> {
  return this.http.get('/api/data').toPromise();
}
```

When the server responds with `)]}',\n["key": "value"]`, HttpClient will strip the prefix and parse only the valid JSON portion. This is built into the response parsing pipeline.

To protect API endpoints from XSSI attacks manually, you need to prepend the non-executable prefix `)]}',\n` to all JSON responses. In Node.js Express, configure this middleware before your route handlers.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (res.getHeader('Content-Type') && res.getHeader('Content-Type').includes('application/json')) {
    res.setHeader('X-XSSI-Protection', 'enabled');
  }
  next();
});

app.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {
  const data = { message: 'Hello World' };
  res.set('Content-Type', 'application/json');
  res.send(')]}', '\n' + JSON.stringify(data));
});
```

Client-side code should strip the prefix before parsing:
```javascript
const response = await fetch('/api/data');
const text = await response.text();
const jsonText = text.replace(/^\)\]}'\n/, '');
const data = JSON.parse(jsonText);
```

You can create an HTTP response interceptor to detect and log XSSI prefixes during development. This helps verify your API endpoints are properly configured.

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpInterceptor, HttpRequest, HttpResponse } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable()
export class XSSIDebugInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpInterceptor): Observable<HttpResponse<any>> {
    return next.handle(req).subscribe({
      next: (response: HttpResponse<any>) => {
        if (response.headers.get('Content-Type')?.includes('application/json')) {
          const body = response.body;
          if (body && typeof body === 'string' && body.startsWith(')]}', '\n')) {
            console.log('[XSSI DETECTED] Prefix found in response:', response.status);
          }
        }
      }
    });
  }
}
```

Register this interceptor in your app module:
```typescript
import { HTTP_INTERCEPTORS } from '@angular/common/http';

@NgModule({
  providers: [
    {
      provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
      useClass: XSSIDebugInterceptor,
      multi: true
    }
  ]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

No. By default, the interceptor sends the `X-XSRF-TOKEN` header only on mutating requests (such as POST, PUT, DELETE) to relative and same origin URLs. It does not send this header on GET or HEAD requests because CSRF protection is only needed for requests that can change state on the backend.

```typescript
// The interceptor logic applies automatically when using HttpClient
// It reads from cookie: XSRF-TOKEN
// It sets header: X-XSRF-TOKEN
// Scope: Mutating requests (POST, PUT, etc.)
```

Ensure your server is configured to set the token in a JavaScript readable session cookie called `XSRF-TOKEN` on either the page load or the first GET request.

To prevent collisions in environments where multiple Angular apps share the same domain, you must give each application a unique cookie name. Your backend service must be configured to set the specific cookie for your page and verify that the header is present on all eligible requests.

```typescript
// Example: Customizing interceptor configuration if defaults are insufficient
// Note: Backend must match the cookie name defined here
const xsrfInterceptor = new XsrfInterceptor({
  cookieName: 'APP1_XSRF_TOKEN', // Unique per application
  headerName: 'X-XSRF-TOKEN'
});
```

The token must be unique for each user and verifiable by the server (e.g., digest of site's authentication cookie with a salt). Failing to configure the backend to verify the header renders Angular's default protection ineffective.

You need to use `withXsrfConfiguration` inside your `provideHttpClient` call to override the default values. Add this provider configuration to your application setup:
```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideHttpClient(
      withXsrfConfiguration({
        cookieName: 'CUSTOM_XSRF_TOKEN',
        headerName: 'X-Custom-Xsrf-Header',
      }),
    ),
  ],
};
```

To disable the built-in Cross-Site Request Forgery (XSRF) protection mechanism globally, you need to configure the ApplicationConfig providers using the provideHttpClient function combined with the withNoXsrfProtection() feature. Here is the implementation pattern:

```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(withNoXsrfProtection())],
};
```
This configuration ensures that all HTTP requests made through the Angular HttpClient bypass standard XSRF token validation.

Internationalization (i18n) is the process of designing and preparing your project for use in different locales around the world. Localization is the subsequent process of building specific versions of that project for those different locales. The localization process includes two main actions: extracting text for translation into different languages and formatting data for a specific locale.

```typescript
// Conceptual example of extraction logic structure
const extractedTexts = {
  greeting: 'Hello',
  currencyLabel: 'Price'
};

// Localization action: Formatting data for a specific locale
function formatData(value, locale) {
  // Implementation depends on the specific locale requirements
}
```

A locale identifies a region where people speak a particular language or variant, such as a country or geographical area. It determines the formatting and parsing of measurement units including date and time, numbers, and currencies. Additionally, it controls translated names for time zones, languages, and countries.

To implement this behavior using standard JavaScript APIs that align with Angular i18n concepts:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class LocaleService {
  formatCurrency(amount: number, locale: string): string {
    const formatter = new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {
      style: 'currency',
      currency: 'USD'
    });
    return formatter.format(amount);
  }

  formatDate(date: Date, locale: string): string {
    const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale);
    return formatter.format(date);
  }
}
```
